# ft_printf

## Description

**ft_printf** is a recode of the libc `printf()`. The goal is to build a static library, `libftprintf.a`, that prints with `write()` and handles the conversions below. It returns the number of characters printed.

```c
int ft_printf(const char *, ...);
```

| Conversion | Description |
|:----------:|-------------|
| `%c` | A single character |
| `%s` | A string (`(null)` if NULL) |
| `%p` | A pointer in hexadecimal, prefixed with `0x` (`(nil)` if NULL) |
| `%d` `%i` | A signed decimal number |
| `%u` | An unsigned decimal number |
| `%x` `%X` | Hexadecimal, lowercase / uppercase |
| `%%` | A percent sign |

## Instructions

```bash
make        # builds libftprintf.a (using ar)
make clean  # removes object files
make fclean # removes object files and the library
make re     # fclean, then all
```

```bash
cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror main.c libftprintf.a -o program
```

## Algorithm and Data Structure

**Algorithm:** the format string is read once, left to right. A normal character is written directly. After a `%`, the next character selects the function to call, and each function returns the number of characters it wrote. The total is returned at the end.

**Data structures:** `va_list` to read the arguments, and an `int` counter for the return value.

## Resources

- `man 3 printf`, `man 3 stdarg`, `man 2 write`, `man ar`
- https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/variadic

**Use of AI:** AI was used to understand `va_list` (parsing part), to generate test cases such as `INT_MIN`, `NULL` and `0` (testing part), and to proofread this README. The code was written, tested and understood by me.